smells.draft_prompt:
Detect the "Unnecessary Iteration" code smell.

Definition:
This smell occurs when code explicitly iterates over data in a Pandas DataFrame or Series even though the same operation can reasonably be expressed using a Pandas built-in or vectorized operation.

The smell is about unnecessary iteration, not about loops in general.

Detection rules:
- Report loops that iterate over Pandas data row by row or element by element when an equivalent Pandas or vectorized operation can perform the same computation.
- Relevant patterns may include:
      for index, row in df.iterrows():
      for row in df.itertuples():
      for i in range(len(df)):
      for i in df.index:
      for value in df["column"]:
  but these patterns are not automatically smells. Inspect the loop body before deciding.

Typical smelly cases include loops used to:
- compute or transform DataFrame column values independently for each row;
- manually filter rows according to a condition;
- manually aggregate values when a Pandas aggregation is available;
- populate a new column one element at a time;
- create a list or DataFrame through row-wise processing that can be replaced by a vectorized expression;
- perform arithmetic or Boolean operations independently on every row.

Example:
    result = []
    for index, row in df.iterrows():
        result.append(row["value"] + 1)

A vectorized alternative exists:
    result = df["value"] + 1

Do NOT report:
- loops over epochs, batches, files, models, neural-network layers, hyperparameters, folds, or other non-Pandas objects;
- loops whose iterations depend on results produced by previous iterations;
- loops involving essential external side effects;
- complex algorithms for which there is no reasonably equivalent Pandas operation;
- loops where a DataFrame is merely accessed inside the body but the loop itself is not iterating over DataFrame data;
- a loop only because iterrows(), itertuples(), or range(len(df)) appears. Confirm that the work performed by the loop can actually be replaced by an appropriate Pandas/vectorized operation.

Use the complete file to infer whether variables are Pandas DataFrames or Series.

Be conservative: report the smell only when a reasonable vectorized or Pandas API alternative exists.
